Our earlier horizontal example, which had an unintended collision, was correctly rejected. The validator found that intersection at 1250 ms.
Searching for new levels
Remove --check and add --top 10 to display the ten candidates with the greatest minimum separation:
python3 colliding_pixels_generator.py \
    --a 7 2 --b 2 10 \
    --mirror-a 1 0 --mirror-b 0 0 \
    --time 3000 --top 10


You can also adjust the visual requirements:
Option	Default	Purpose
--window	200 ms	Final approach window
--clearance	2.0 px	Minimum separation before that window
--top	10	Number of candidates to display
--width, --height	30	Panel dimensions

The script prints ready-to-paste C++ for each candidate.

One limitation worth keeping in mind: the mathematical validator treats pixels as points. The clearance threshold approximates visual separation, 
but it doesn't yet simulate how your framebuffer rounds positions onto LEDs. Also, the early-intersection test excludes the final 1ms to distinguish 
the intended collision from the continuous approach to it.
